from full_a import C
LONG = {}

LONG["jacob"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Jacob was", """Jacob was the grandson of Abraham and the son of Isaac and Rebekah. He lived about 1800 BC in Canaan. He and his twin brother Esau were born after Rebekah had struggled to have children and Isaac had prayed for her. Before they were born, God told Rebekah, “Two nations are in your womb... the older shall serve the younger.”

Esau came first, red and hairy. Jacob came right after, holding on to Esau’s heel. So he was named Jacob, which means “he grasps the heel,” and also “he cheats.” That name described a good part of his early life.""", ("Genesis", 25)),
("Why his story matters", """Jacob’s story is about a flawed man whom God kept pursuing. God changed his name to Israel, and from his twelve sons came the twelve tribes of the nation of Israel.""")],
cards=[
C("The birthright and the blessing", "Jacob bought Esau’s birthright for a bowl of stew, and later tricked his blind father to get the blessing.",
"""Esau was a hunter and his father’s favorite. Jacob was quiet and stayed in the tents, and was his mother’s favorite. One day Esau came home from the field exhausted and hungry. Jacob was cooking red lentil stew. Esau said, “Let me eat some of that red stuff.” Jacob said, “Sell me your birthright now.” The birthright belonged to the firstborn and meant a double share of the inheritance and leadership of the family. Esau said, “I am about to die; what good is a birthright to me?” He swore an oath and sold it. “Thus Esau despised his birthright.”

Years later, when Isaac was old and blind, he told Esau to hunt wild game and prepare a meal, and then he would give him the blessing. Rebekah overheard and told Jacob to pretend to be Esau. She cooked a meal, put goatskins on Jacob’s hands and neck, and dressed him in Esau’s clothes. Isaac was suspicious: “The voice is Jacob’s voice, but the hands are the hands of Esau.” He blessed Jacob. When Esau returned, he cried out with a loud and bitter cry. Isaac said, “Your brother came deceitfully, and he has taken away your blessing.” Esau planned to kill Jacob, so Rebekah sent him away to her brother Laban in Haran.""", ("Genesis", 27)),
C("A ladder at Bethel", "On his first night running away, Jacob dreamed of a ladder to heaven and heard God’s promise.",
"""Jacob left home with nothing but a staff. On the first night, he lay down with a stone for a pillow. “And he dreamed, and behold, there was a ladder set up on the earth, and the top of it reached to heaven. And behold, the angels of God were ascending and descending on it!” The Lord stood above it and said, “I am the Lord, the God of Abraham your father and the God of Isaac. The land on which you lie I will give to you and to your offspring... Behold, I am with you and will keep you wherever you go, and will bring you back to this land.”

Jacob woke up, afraid. “Surely the Lord is in this place, and I did not know it... This is none other than the house of God, and this is the gate of heaven.” He set up the stone as a pillar, poured oil on it and called the place Bethel, “house of God.” He made a vow: if God would be with him and bring him back safely, “then the Lord shall be my God.” God had chosen him not because he was good but because of His own promise.""", ("Genesis", 28)),
C("Laban, Leah and Rachel", "Jacob worked fourteen years to marry Rachel, after being tricked into marrying Leah first.",
"""In Haran, Jacob met Rachel, his cousin, at a well, and fell in love. He agreed to work seven years for her father Laban in exchange for marrying her. “So Jacob served seven years for Rachel, and they seemed to him but a few days because of the love he had for her.” On the wedding night, Laban gave him Rachel’s older sister, Leah, instead, under the veil in the dark. In the morning Jacob said, “What is this you have done to me?” The deceiver had been deceived. Laban said that in his country the older was married first. He offered Rachel as well if Jacob worked another seven years.

Jacob’s household became a story of rivalry. Leah had many sons, and Rachel was barren for a long time. Their servants Bilhah and Zilpah also had sons. Eventually Jacob had twelve sons and a daughter, Dinah. Rachel finally gave birth to Joseph. Jacob worked for Laban another six years for flocks of his own, and Laban kept changing his wages. By then Jacob had grown wealthy, and Laban’s sons were jealous. God told Jacob it was time to go home.""", ("Genesis", 29)),
C("Wrestling with God", "Jacob wrestled all night with a man who was God. He was given a new name, Israel.",
"""Jacob was returning to Canaan with his family and flocks, but he was afraid of meeting Esau. He heard that Esau was coming with 400 men. Jacob sent gifts ahead of him: hundreds of goats, sheep, camels, cows and donkeys. He prayed: “O God of my father Abraham... I am not worthy of the least of all the deeds of steadfast love and all the faithfulness that you have shown to your servant... Please deliver me from the hand of my brother.”

That night, he sent his family across the river Jabbok and stayed behind alone. “And a man wrestled with him until the breaking of the day.” When the man saw that he did not prevail, he touched Jacob’s hip socket and put it out of joint. “Let me go, for the day has broken.” Jacob said, “I will not let you go unless you bless me.” “What is your name?” “Jacob.” “Your name shall no longer be called Jacob, but Israel, for you have striven with God and with men, and have prevailed.” Jacob named the place Peniel, “the face of God.” “I have seen God face to face, and yet my life has been delivered.” He limped away.""", ("Genesis", 32)),
C("Meeting Esau", "Esau ran to meet Jacob, hugged him and wept. Twenty years of fear ended in forgiveness.",
"""Jacob saw Esau coming with his 400 men. He divided his family, putting the servants first, then Leah and her children, and Rachel and Joseph last. He went ahead of all of them and bowed seven times. “But Esau ran to meet him and embraced him and fell on his neck and kissed him, and they wept.”

Jacob had expected revenge. He found forgiveness. He said, “To see your face is like seeing the face of God, and you have received me favorably.” Esau at first refused the gifts. “I have enough, my brother.” Jacob insisted, and Esau accepted them. Esau had his own large family and had become wealthy. Jacob’s struggle had been long, but at the end God had not only kept His word but also softened the heart of the brother he had wronged.""", ("Genesis", 33)),
C("Loss and Egypt", "Rachel died giving birth to Benjamin. Jacob lost Joseph, but years later he was reunited with him in Egypt.",
"""Back in Canaan, Jacob’s life was full of heartbreak. His daughter Dinah was assaulted, and his sons took violent revenge. Rachel died giving birth to Benjamin near Bethlehem, and Jacob set up a pillar on her grave. His older sons sold Joseph into slavery, and for twenty-two years Jacob believed him dead. When famine came, he sent his sons to Egypt for food, and Joseph was found alive.

“It is enough; Joseph my son is still alive. I will go and see him before I die.” Jacob traveled to Egypt at 130. Pharaoh asked his age and Jacob said, “The days of the years of my sojourning are 130 years. Few and evil have been the days of the years of my life.” Before he died at 147, Jacob blessed Joseph’s two sons and each of his own sons, and he told them to bury him in the cave of Machpelah with Abraham and Isaac. The Bible says Joseph and his brothers carried him there. The promises of God to Abraham, Isaac and Jacob would be carried on by the twelve tribes of Israel.""", ("Genesis", 47))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Jacob’s life is a long story of struggle. He lied, was lied to, ran, worked, was afraid and was hurt. Yet God stayed with him. At Bethel He promised, “I am with you,” and He kept the promise through twenty years in a foreign land, a night of wrestling and many years of sorrow.

Jacob’s limp is a reminder. He met God and was changed, but also marked. The Bible does not say that a close encounter with God leaves us without scars. It says it leaves us with a new name, a blessing and a new way of living.

And the story shows that God’s choice of Jacob was grace. He did not wait for Jacob to be good. He chose him, and then changed him. If you ever feel that your past disqualifies you, remember the one whose name meant “cheater” and whom God renamed Israel.""", ("Genesis", 32))])

LONG["adam-eve"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Adam and Eve were", """Adam and Eve are the first man and woman in the Bible. The first two chapters of Genesis say God made them at the end of the creation week. “So God created man in his own image, in the image of God he created him; male and female he created them.” Adam’s name is the Hebrew word for “man,” and it is related to the word for “ground,” from which he was formed. Eve’s name means “life,” or “living.”

They lived in a garden God had planted in Eden. The Bible does not say exactly where Eden was, but it mentions four rivers flowing from it, including the Tigris and the Euphrates.""", ("Genesis", 1)),
("Why their story matters", """Almost everything in the Bible depends on this story. It explains who we are, why the world is broken, and why we need a Savior. The New Testament calls Jesus “the last Adam.”""")],
cards=[
C("Made and placed in a garden", "God formed Adam from the dust, breathed life into him, and put him in the garden. He then made Eve.",
"""“Then the Lord God formed the man of dust from the ground and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life, and the man became a living creature.” God planted a garden in Eden with every tree that was pleasing to the eye and good for food. Adam’s job was “to work it and keep it.” The first work in the Bible was a gift, not a punishment.

God gave one rule: Adam could eat from any tree in the garden except the tree of the knowledge of good and evil. “In the day that you eat of it you shall surely die.” Then God said, “It is not good that the man should be alone.” He brought the animals to Adam to name, but none was a suitable partner. So God put Adam into a deep sleep, took one of his ribs, or a part of his side, and made a woman. Adam said, “This at last is bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh.” “And the man and his wife were both naked and were not ashamed.” It was a world of harmony: with God, with each other and with creation.""", ("Genesis", 2)),
C("The serpent’s question", "A serpent asked Eve, “Did God actually say...?” She ate the fruit and gave some to Adam.",
"""Then comes the serpent, “more crafty than any other beast of the field.” Later in the Bible, the serpent is identified with Satan. He asked the woman, “Did God actually say, ‘You shall not eat of any tree in the garden’?” He twisted God’s words. Eve answered that they could eat of all the trees but one, and God said they must not touch it or they would die. The serpent said, “You will not surely die. For God knows that when you eat of it your eyes will be opened, and you will be like God, knowing good and evil.”

He suggested that God was holding something back, and that God could not be trusted. “So when the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was a delight to the eyes, and that the tree was to be desired to make one wise, she took of its fruit and ate, and she also gave some to her husband who was with her, and he ate.” The Bible does not say what kind of fruit it was. The idea that it was an apple comes from later tradition.""", ("Genesis", 3)),
C("Hiding and blame", "Adam and Eve felt shame and hid. When God asked what happened, each blamed someone else.",
"""“Then the eyes of both were opened, and they knew that they were naked. And they sewed fig leaves together and made themselves loincloths.” That evening they heard the Lord God walking in the garden and hid among the trees. The Lord called, “Where are you?” God knew where they were. The question gave Adam a chance to come out.

Adam said, “I heard the sound of you in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked, and I hid myself.” God asked, “Have you eaten of the tree of which I commanded you not to eat?” Adam answered, “The woman whom you gave to be with me, she gave me fruit of the tree, and I ate.” He blamed Eve and, indirectly, God. Eve said, “The serpent deceived me, and I ate.” Neither said, “I did wrong.” Sin brought fear, shame, blame and distance from God, and it has been with us ever since.""", ("Genesis", 3)),
C("Consequences and a promise", "God described the consequences of their choice. But He also made a promise that someone would crush the serpent.",
"""God spoke to the serpent first, cursing him. Then He gave a promise that has been called the first gospel: “I will put enmity between you and the woman, and between your offspring and her offspring; he shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise his heel.” Someone born of the woman would one day defeat the serpent, though it would cost him a wound. The New Testament says this points to Jesus.

To the woman, God said that childbirth would be painful and her relationship with her husband would be strained. To Adam, He said that the ground would be cursed, and work would be hard: “By the sweat of your face you shall eat bread, till you return to the ground, for out of it you were taken; for you are dust, and to dust you shall return.” Death had entered the world.

Yet even then there is kindness. “And the Lord God made for Adam and for his wife garments of skins and clothed them.” Covering their shame required the death of an animal, the first picture of how God would cover our sin.""", ("Genesis", 3)),
C("Out of the garden", "God sent Adam and Eve out of Eden and guarded the way back to the tree of life.",
"""God said, “Behold, the man has become like one of us in knowing good and evil. Now, lest he reach out his hand and take also of the tree of life and eat, and live forever...” So He sent them out of the garden. At the east of Eden He placed cherubim, angelic guardians, and a flaming sword that turned in every direction to guard the way to the tree of life. It was a judgment, but also a mercy: they would not live forever in a fallen state.

Outside Eden, Adam and Eve had children. Cain was born first, then Abel. Eve said, “I have gotten a man with the help of the Lord.” After Abel’s death, Seth was born. Adam lived 930 years and had other sons and daughters. The Bible says only that “he died.” The tree of life shows up again in the last chapters of the Bible, in the new Jerusalem, where God’s people will once again be able to eat of it.""", ("Genesis", 5))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """The story of Adam and Eve explains many things we know from experience. We were made for a close relationship with God, and something has gone wrong. We hide, blame and cover ourselves. We know what we should do, and we do not. The Bible says this goes back to the first choice to doubt God’s word and take life into our own hands.

But the story also gives hope. God came looking for them in the garden: “Where are you?” He still does. He gave them clothes, and He gave them a promise. From the very beginning, the plan of rescue was in motion.

The apostle Paul says, “For as in Adam all die, so also in Christ shall all be made alive.” Adam brought death. Jesus brings life. What was lost in the first garden is restored by the Savior who prayed in a garden, died on a cross and rose from a tomb in a garden.""", ("Romans", 5))])

LONG["abraham"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Abraham was", """Abraham, first called Abram, is the father of the Jewish people and a great man of faith for Christians and Muslims also. He lived about 2000 BC in Ur, a prosperous city in what is now southern Iraq, where people worshiped many gods. His father Terah took the family north to Haran. Abram was already married to Sarai, who could not have children.

Everything changed when God spoke to him. It was the beginning of the story of the Bible’s main plot: God’s plan to bless the whole world.""", ("Genesis", 12)),
("Why his story matters", """Abraham’s story is the foundation of the Bible’s teaching about faith. The apostle Paul says that Abraham was made right with God because he believed. He is called “the friend of God.”""")],
cards=[
C("The call", "God told Abram to leave his country and go to a land He would show him. He promised to make him a great nation.",
"""“Now the Lord said to Abram, ‘Go from your country and your kindred and your father’s house to the land that I will show you. And I will make of you a great nation, and I will bless you and make your name great, so that you will be a blessing. I will bless those who bless you, and him who dishonors you I will curse, and in you all the families of the earth shall be blessed.’”

Abram was 75 and childless. He had no map and no idea where he was going. “So Abram went, as the Lord had told him.” He took Sarai, his nephew Lot, his servants and his flocks. The promise included land, descendants and a blessing for all nations, and the Bible’s whole story is about how God made good on it. Hebrews says, “By faith Abraham obeyed when he was called to go out to a place that he was to receive as an inheritance. And he went out, not knowing where he was going.”""", ("Genesis", 12)),
C("Stars and a promise", "Years later, God took Abram outside, told him to count the stars, and promised him descendants as numerous.",
"""Abram arrived in Canaan and lived as a nomad in tents. He and his nephew Lot had grown so rich that they needed to separate. Abram generously let Lot choose first, and Lot chose the rich Jordan valley near Sodom. Abram stayed in the hills. Soon after, Lot was taken captive in a war between local kings. Abram led 318 trained men and rescued him. After the battle, a mysterious priest-king named Melchizedek blessed him.

Years went by and still there was no child. Abram complained to God. God took him outside: “Look toward heaven, and number the stars, if you are able to number them... So shall your offspring be.” Then comes one of the most important verses in the Bible: “And he believed the Lord, and he counted it to him as righteousness.” Abram was not made right with God by his good works, but by trusting God’s promise. The Bible gives that as the pattern of salvation, for Jews and non-Jews.""", ("Genesis", 15)),
C("Waiting, laughing and interceding", "God changed his name to Abraham, “father of many.” He argued with God about Sodom.",
"""When Abram was 99, God appeared again and changed his name to Abraham, “father of a multitude.” He gave circumcision as the sign of the covenant. He also said Sarah would have a son within a year. Abraham fell on his face and laughed, “Shall a child be born to a man who is a hundred years old?” God told him to name the child Isaac, “he laughs.”

Soon after, the Lord visited Abraham with two angels and told him that He was about to destroy the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah, where Lot lived. Abraham stepped forward and argued: “Will you indeed sweep away the righteous with the wicked?... Shall not the Judge of all the earth do what is just?” He kept asking: if there were fifty righteous people, forty-five, forty, thirty, twenty, ten? Each time, God agreed. Not even ten were found. But God rescued Lot and his two daughters before the cities were destroyed. Abraham’s boldness shows that God invites our prayers.""", ("Genesis", 18)),
C("Isaac, and the hardest test", "Abraham finally had his son Isaac. Then God asked him to offer Isaac as a sacrifice.",
"""When Abraham was 100 and Sarah 90, Isaac was born. The long wait was over. Years later, “God tested Abraham and said to him, ‘Take your son, your only son Isaac, whom you love, and go to the land of Moriah, and offer him there as a burnt offering on one of the mountains of which I shall tell you.’”

Abraham rose early the next morning, took Isaac, two servants, wood and fire and set out. After three days, he saw the place. He told the servants, “Stay here with the donkey; I and the boy will go over there and worship and come again to you.” Isaac carried the wood. “Where is the lamb for a burnt offering?” “God will provide for himself the lamb for a burnt offering, my son.” Abraham built the altar, bound Isaac and raised his knife. The angel of the Lord called from heaven, “Abraham, Abraham! Do not lay your hand on the boy.” Abraham saw a ram caught in a thicket and offered it instead. He called the place “The Lord will provide.”

Hebrews says Abraham “considered that God was able even to raise him from the dead.” The story points ahead to God’s own Son, the Lamb whom God provided.""", ("Genesis", 22)),
C("A wife for Isaac", "After Sarah’s death, Abraham sent his servant to find a wife for Isaac. Rebekah was chosen.",
"""When Sarah died, Abraham was 137. He bought the cave of Machpelah near Hebron for her burial. Then he began to think about Isaac’s future. He did not want Isaac to marry a Canaanite woman. He sent his most trusted servant back to his relatives in Mesopotamia to find a wife.

At the well outside the town of Nahor, the servant prayed that the woman who offered water to him and his camels would be the one. Before he finished praying, Rebekah came and did exactly that. She was Abraham’s great-niece. She agreed to go. When she first saw Isaac walking in the field, she covered herself with a veil. “Then Isaac brought her into the tent of Sarah his mother, and took Rebekah, and she became his wife, and he loved her.”""", ("Genesis", 24)),
C("Death and legacy", "Abraham died at 175 and was buried next to Sarah. His descendants include Israel, the Arabs and, through Jesus, all believers.",
"""Abraham took another wife, Keturah, and had six more sons. But he left everything to Isaac. He died “in a good old age, an old man and full of years,” at 175. His sons Isaac and Ishmael buried him together in the cave of Machpelah with Sarah.

The promise to Abraham echoes through the whole Bible. The nation of Israel came from him through Isaac and Jacob. The Arab nations trace their origin through Ishmael. And the New Testament teaches that all who believe in Jesus are Abraham’s spiritual children: “Know then that it is those of faith who are the sons of Abraham... And the Scripture, foreseeing that God would justify the Gentiles by faith, preached the gospel beforehand to Abraham, saying, ‘In you shall all the nations be blessed.’” Abraham is also called “the friend of God.”""", ("Galatians", 3))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Abraham’s story shows how faith works. He did not know where he was going. He did not understand everything God asked. But he took God at His word and acted. His faith was not perfect: he lied about Sarah and tried to help God with Hagar. But he trusted, and God counted it as righteousness.

His story also shows that God keeps promises. It took 25 years for Isaac to be born, and many centuries for the promise of blessing for all nations to come to full effect. God works on a long timeline, and we can trust Him.

Finally, the ram on Mount Moriah shows that God provides. At the moment of greatest need, a substitute was ready. The apostle Paul says that God “did not spare his own Son but gave him up for us all.” “The Lord will provide” is true in the biggest sense.""", ("Romans", 4))])

LONG["bartholomew"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Bartholomew was", """Bartholomew is one of the twelve apostles. The name is not really a first name. It means “son of Tolmai,” so it was a family name, and he probably had another name. In Matthew, Mark and Luke, Bartholomew is always listed right after Philip. In John’s Gospel, Philip is paired with a man named Nathanael. For that reason, many believe Bartholomew and Nathanael are the same man. The Bible does not say so directly, but it fits the evidence.

Nathanael came from Cana, a village in Galilee, where Jesus later turned water into wine.""", ("John", 1)),
("Why his story matters", """Nathanael’s first meeting with Jesus is one of the most memorable in the Gospels. It shows how Jesus knows people, and how someone with doubts can become a believer in one conversation.""")],
cards=[
C("Philip’s invitation", "Philip found Nathanael and told him they had found the Messiah. Nathanael said, “Can anything good come out of Nazareth?”",
"""Jesus had just called Philip: “Follow me.” Philip went straight to his friend. “Philip found Nathanael and said to him, ‘We have found him of whom Moses in the Law and also the prophets wrote, Jesus of Nazareth, the son of Joseph.’” For a pious Jew who read the Law and the Prophets, this was big news.

Nathanael’s response was skeptical, and honest: “Can anything good come out of Nazareth?” Nazareth was a small village of perhaps a few hundred people. It was never mentioned in the Old Testament, and the Messiah was supposed to come from Bethlehem. Cana was only a few miles from Nazareth, so he probably knew exactly what it was like. Philip did not argue. He said, “Come and see.” It is some of the best advice ever given for sharing the Gospel: not winning an argument, but inviting someone to meet Jesus.""", ("John", 1)),
C("“An Israelite indeed”", "When Nathanael approached, Jesus said, “Behold, an Israelite indeed, in whom there is no deceit!”",
"""Nathanael came. Jesus saw him approaching and said, “Behold, an Israelite indeed, in whom there is no deceit!” It was a compliment, and also a small reference to Jacob, the father of Israel, whose name had once meant “deceiver.” Nathanael was a true Israelite: straightforward and honest, with no pretense. He said what he thought.

Nathanael was surprised. “How do you know me?” Jesus answered, “Before Philip called you, when you were under the fig tree, I saw you.” The Bible does not say what Nathanael was doing under the fig tree. People often studied or prayed in the shade of a fig tree. Whatever it was, it was private, and something that touched him deeply. Jesus knew about a moment no one else had seen.""", ("John", 1)),
C("“You are the Son of God”", "Nathanael instantly declared that Jesus was the Son of God and King of Israel. Jesus promised he would see greater things.",
"""Nathanael’s doubts vanished. “Rabbi, you are the Son of God! You are the King of Israel!” It is one of the strongest statements of faith by any disciple in the first chapter of John. A man who had questioned whether anything good could come from Nazareth was now calling Jesus the Son of God.

Jesus answered, “Because I said to you, ‘I saw you under the fig tree,’ do you believe? You will see greater things than these.” Then He said, “Truly, truly, I say to you, you will see heaven opened, and the angels of God ascending and descending on the Son of Man.” That was a reference to Jacob’s dream at Bethel, a ladder to heaven. Jesus was saying that He Himself is the connection between heaven and earth. Nathanael would see that more fully in the years that followed.""", ("John", 1)),
C("Fishing and the upper room", "Nathanael was with the disciples when the risen Jesus appeared at the Sea of Galilee. He was with the apostles in the upper room.",
"""Nathanael is mentioned only a few more times. In John’s last chapter, after the resurrection, seven disciples are listed together: “Simon Peter, Thomas (called the Twin), Nathanael of Cana in Galilee, the sons of Zebedee, and two others of his disciples.” They had gone fishing all night and caught nothing. In the morning, a man on the shore told them to cast the net on the right side, and the net was so full they could not haul it in. “It is the Lord!” John said. Jesus had breakfast ready on the beach.

Later, when the apostles were in Jerusalem waiting for the Holy Spirit, Acts lists Bartholomew among them. Then the Bible’s record ends. Later traditions say that he preached in places such as Armenia and India and died as a martyr. The Bible does not tell us. What is clear is that the doubter from Cana became a witness of the risen Lord.""", ("John", 21))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Nathanael’s story shows that honest doubt is no barrier to faith. He asked a blunt question and received an answer. Jesus does not mind questions. He welcomes people who are honest.

“Come and see” is still the best invitation. Philip didn’t have a full argument for Nathanael. He had a friend who had met Jesus, and he said, “Come and see.” If you have a friend who has doubts, this might be the best approach.

Finally, Jesus’ words, “I saw you under the fig tree,” are a comfort. He sees us in places no one else does. He knew Nathanael before Philip called him, and He knows each of us. Nothing about us is hidden from Him, and He still invites us to follow.""", ("John", 1))])

LONG["john-baptist"] = dict(intro=[
("Who John the Baptist was", """John was the son of a priest named Zechariah and his wife Elizabeth, a relative of Mary, the mother of Jesus. He was born about six months before Jesus, in the hill country of Judea. His parents were old and had never been able to have children. The angel Gabriel announced his birth to Zechariah in the Temple in Jerusalem and said that John would be “great before the Lord” and “filled with the Holy Spirit, even from his mother’s womb.”

Zechariah did not believe the angel and was struck mute until the baby was born. When John was eight days old and people expected the family name, Elizabeth said, “No; he shall be called John.” Zechariah wrote, “His name is John,” and his tongue was loosed. He praised God and prophesied that his son would be “the prophet of the Most High” and would “go before the Lord to prepare his ways.”""", ("Luke", 1)),
("Why his story matters", """John is the last of the Old Testament-style prophets and the first witness to Jesus. Jesus said, “Among those born of women there has arisen no one greater than John the Baptist.” His life shows what it means to point away from yourself and toward Jesus.""")],
cards=[
C("A voice in the wilderness", "John lived in the desert, dressed like Elijah, and called the nation to repent.",
"""The Bible tells us almost nothing about John’s childhood, except that he “grew and became strong in spirit, and he was in the wilderness until the day of his public appearance to Israel.” He appeared around AD 26, in the fifteenth year of the emperor Tiberius. “Now John wore a garment of camel’s hair and a leather belt around his waist, and his food was locusts and wild honey.” It was the dress of the prophet Elijah.

He preached near the Jordan River: “Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.” The prophet Isaiah had foretold him: “The voice of one crying in the wilderness: ‘Prepare the way of the Lord; make his paths straight.’” People came from Jerusalem and all Judea and were baptized by him in the Jordan, confessing their sins. John was blunt. He told the religious leaders, “You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the wrath to come? Bear fruit in keeping with repentance.”""", ("Matthew", 3)),
C("What should we do?", "Crowds, tax collectors and soldiers asked John what to do. His answers were simple and practical.",
"""People asked, “What then shall we do?” John said, “Whoever has two tunics is to share with him who has none, and whoever has food is to do likewise.” Tax collectors asked what they should do: “Collect no more than you are authorized to do.” Soldiers asked, “And we, what shall we do?” “Do not extort money from anyone by threats or by false accusation, and be content with your wages.”

He did not tell them to leave their jobs and live in the desert. He told them to be honest and generous right where they were. People wondered whether John himself was the Messiah. He said, “I baptize you with water for repentance, but he who is coming after me is mightier than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to carry. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.” He did not take any of the credit.""", ("Luke", 3)),
C("Baptizing Jesus", "Jesus came to John to be baptized. John said, “Behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world!”",
"""One day Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan to be baptized by John. John tried to stop Him. “I need to be baptized by you, and do you come to me?” Jesus said, “Let it be so now, for thus it is fitting for us to fulfill all righteousness.” When He came up out of the water, the heavens opened. The Spirit of God descended like a dove and rested on Him, and a voice from heaven said, “This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased.”

The next day, John saw Jesus walking toward him and said, “Behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world!” Two of John’s disciples, Andrew and probably John the apostle, heard him and followed Jesus. John did not hold them back. As more people started going to Jesus, John’s disciples complained. He said, “He must increase, but I must decrease.” John described himself as the friend of the bridegroom, happy just to hear the groom’s voice.""", ("John", 1)),
C("Prison and doubt", "John confronted King Herod Antipas and was thrown in prison. There he began to wonder if Jesus was really the one.",
"""Herod Antipas, ruler of Galilee, had married Herodias, the wife of his own brother Philip. John told him publicly, “It is not lawful for you to have her.” Herodias hated John. Herod arrested him and put him in prison, according to the historian Josephus in the fortress of Machaerus, east of the Dead Sea. Herod liked to listen to John, but his wife wanted him dead.

In prison, John heard about the things Jesus was doing. He had expected a Messiah who would judge and set things right, but Jesus was healing and teaching, and John was in a cell. He sent two disciples to ask, “Are you the one who is to come, or shall we look for another?” Jesus said, “Go and tell John what you hear and see: the blind receive their sight and the lame walk, lepers are cleansed and the deaf hear, and the dead are raised up, and the poor have good news preached to them. And blessed is the one who is not offended by me.” Jesus did not scold John for doubting. After the messengers left, He praised John in front of the crowd: “Among those born of women there has arisen no one greater than John the Baptist.”""", ("Matthew", 11)),
C("The death of John", "At a birthday banquet, Herod’s stepdaughter danced and asked for John’s head. Herod ordered his execution.",
"""On Herod’s birthday, he held a banquet for his nobles and officers. Herodias’ daughter danced for the guests, and Herod was so pleased that he promised, with an oath, to give her whatever she asked, up to half of his kingdom. She went to her mother. “What should I ask?” “The head of John the Baptist.” She went back and said, “I want you to give me at once the head of John the Baptist on a platter.”

“And the king was exceedingly sorry, but because of his oaths and his guests he did not want to break his word to her.” He sent an executioner, who beheaded John in the prison and brought his head on a platter to the girl, who gave it to her mother. John’s disciples came and took his body and laid it in a tomb. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus withdrew to a quiet place by Himself.

John died young, probably in his early thirties, having fulfilled the work for which he was sent: he had prepared the way.""", ("Mark", 6))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """John’s whole life was pointing to someone else. “He must increase, but I must decrease.” In a world that wants us to be the center of attention, John shows what it looks like to find joy in pointing people to Jesus.

He also shows courage. He told the truth to ordinary people and to the king, and it cost him his life. He did not have to be popular to be faithful.

And the story of his doubts is a comfort. The greatest of the prophets had questions when he was in prison. Jesus did not condemn him but gave him evidence and encouragement. If you have doubts in a dark time, bring them to Jesus. He knows how to answer honestly and gently.""", ("John", 3))])

LONG["philip"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Philip was", """Philip was one of the twelve apostles. He was from Bethsaida, a fishing village on the north shore of the Sea of Galilee. It was the hometown of Andrew and Peter as well. Philip has a Greek name, and Bethsaida was a mixed area, so he probably spoke both Greek and Aramaic. This was important later in the story.

This is not the same man as Philip the evangelist in the book of Acts, who was one of the seven chosen with Stephen. This is Philip the apostle.""", ("John", 1)),
("Why his story matters", """Philip shows up at key moments in John’s Gospel, always as a practical, down-to-earth person with honest questions. His story shows that Jesus is patient with the disciples who are a little slow and a little literal.""")],
cards=[
C("“Follow me”", "Jesus found Philip and called him. Philip immediately went to tell his friend Nathanael.",
"""The day after Jesus called Andrew and Peter, “Jesus decided to go to Galilee. He found Philip and said to him, ‘Follow me.’” The Gospel of John says that Jesus found him. Philip had been in the same circle of John the Baptist’s followers, and he was ready.

He did not keep it to himself. He found Nathanael and said, “We have found him of whom Moses in the Law and also the prophets wrote, Jesus of Nazareth, the son of Joseph.” When Nathanael doubted, Philip simply said, “Come and see.” It is the first example of a disciple bringing a friend to Jesus, and it is a good one: no pressure, no long arguments, an honest invitation.""", ("John", 1)),
C("Where will we buy bread?", "A crowd of thousands was hungry. Jesus asked Philip where to buy food, to test him.",
"""Jesus went up a mountain near the Sea of Galilee, and a large crowd followed. Looking up, He saw them coming and said to Philip, “Where are we to buy bread, so that these people may eat?” John explains, “He said this to test him, for he himself knew what he would do.”

Why Philip? He was from Bethsaida, near the area, so he would have known the local towns and markets. Philip did the math: “Two hundred denarii worth of bread would not be enough for each of them to get a little.” Two hundred denarii was about eight months of a laborer’s wages. Philip saw the problem clearly, but he did not think about what Jesus could do. Andrew pointed out a boy with five barley loaves and two fish. Jesus took them, gave thanks, and fed the five thousand. Philip got to distribute the bread.""", ("John", 6)),
C("Greeks who wanted to see Jesus", "Some Greek visitors came to Philip and asked, “Sir, we wish to see Jesus.”",
"""During the last week before the crucifixion, Jesus was in Jerusalem for Passover. Some Greeks, non-Jews who had come to worship at the feast, came to Philip. “Sir, we wish to see Jesus.” Of all the twelve, they chose Philip, perhaps because of his Greek name and his background in a mixed region.

Philip did not know what to do. He went and told Andrew, and the two went together to tell Jesus. Jesus answered, “The hour has come for the Son of Man to be glorified. Truly, truly, I say to you, unless a grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies, it remains alone; but if it dies, it bears much fruit.” The arrival of the Gentiles was a sign that the time of His death had come, and that through it the message would go to the whole world. Philip had helped open the door.""", ("John", 12)),
C("“Show us the Father”", "At the Last Supper, Philip said, “Lord, show us the Father, and it is enough for us.”",
"""At the Last Supper, Jesus told the disciples, “If you had known me, you would have known my Father also. From now on you do know him and have seen him.” Philip said what many were probably thinking: “Lord, show us the Father, and it is enough for us.” He wanted to see God.

Jesus answered patiently but with real feeling: “Have I been with you so long, and you still do not know me, Philip? Whoever has seen me has seen the Father. How can you say, ‘Show us the Father’? Do you not believe that I am in the Father and the Father is in me?” He had been with them three years. The answer to Philip’s question was standing in front of him. To see Jesus is to see what God is like: His kindness, His truth, His willingness to wash feet and to give His life.

Philip’s question gave us one of the clearest statements in the Bible about who Jesus is.""", ("John", 14)),
C("After the resurrection", "Philip was in the upper room waiting for the Holy Spirit. Later tradition says he preached in Asia Minor.",
"""After the resurrection, Philip was with the other apostles. Acts lists him in the upper room in Jerusalem, devoted to prayer. At Pentecost, he received the Holy Spirit like the others.

The Bible does not say where Philip went after that. Early traditions say that he preached in Phrygia, in what is now western Turkey, and that he died in the city of Hierapolis, where a church was later built in his memory. The Bible does not confirm it. What we know is that he began with a simple invitation, “Come and see,” and ended as one of the people who carried the Gospel into the world.""", ("Acts", 1))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Philip was not the brightest or the boldest of the twelve. He was a practical man who counted the cost, asked honest questions and brought people to Jesus. Jesus used him in all those ways. You do not have to have all the answers.

His question at the Last Supper is one that people still ask: “Show us the Father.” Jesus’ answer is clear: look at Me. If you want to know what God is like, read the Gospels and watch Jesus.

And the simple invitation, “Come and see,” is still the best way to share your faith. Philip used it on Nathanael, and he helped bring the Greeks to Jesus. Many people are one honest invitation away from meeting Him.""", ("John", 14))])
